    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>After defeat in the Second Opium War and amid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, Qing officials set out to “strengthen themselves” by adopting Western arms, ships and industry while keeping the Confucian order.</li><li>Led above all by provincial governors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zongtang">Zuo Zongtang</a>, it built arsenals, shipyards, mines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a>, schools and a modern navy, and sent students abroad.</li><li>Without reform of government, finance or education its gains stayed scattered, and defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1894–1895 exposed the limits of borrowing technology alone.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s first railway in regular service, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> to Wusong, was built by a British firm without the government’s consent and opened in 1876; the Qing authorities bought it in 1877 and had it torn up.</p></aside>
    </section>

