    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a> is created under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> to conduct relations with the Western powers</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1862</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a> opens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to train interpreters, later adding mathematics and astronomy</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1866</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zongtang">Zuo Zongtang</a> founds the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou-navy-yard">Fuzhou Navy Yard</a> and its naval school</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1872</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> Merchants’ Steam Navigation Company is founded; the first Educational Mission students sail for America</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1884</span><span class="rf-text">A French squadron destroys the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> fleet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1888</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a> is formally established under Admiral Ding Ruchang</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1895</span><span class="rf-text">Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the Qing sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-opium-war.jpg" alt="First Opium War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Destroying_Chinese_war_junks,_by_E._Duncan_(1843).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edward Duncan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 1860 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> Qing dynasty since 1644, faced crises from without and within. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> (1839–1842) had ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanjing</a>, which ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong-island">Hong Kong Island</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and opened five ports to foreign trade. In the Second Opium War (1856–1860) British and French forces took the Dagu forts and marched on Beijing, and in October 1860 their troops looted the Yuanmingyuan, the Old Summer Palace, which the British then burned, while the Xianfeng Emperor fled to Rehe beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>. His younger half-brother, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a>, left behind to negotiate, signed the Convention of Peking, which confirmed the Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> of 1858: foreign envoys would reside in Beijing, more ports were opened, and foreigners and missionaries could travel inland.</p><p>At the same time the empire was torn by rebellion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping Rebellion</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> since 1853 and much of the rich Yangzi valley; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nian-rebellion">Nian Rebellion</a> raged north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>, and Muslim revolts broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> and, from 1862, in the north-west. The dynasty’s own Banner and Green Standard armies had failed against them, and the war was being won by new regional armies raised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiang-army">Xiang Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-army">Huai Army</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>. The Huai Army in particular bought Western rifles and artillery and fought beside foreign-officered units such as the Ever Victorious Army around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>. Their commanders saw at first hand what Western guns and steamships could do.</p><p>When the Xianfeng Emperor died at Rehe in 1861, a palace coup gave power to his widow, the Empress Dowager Ci’an, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>, mother of the five-year-old Tongzhi Emperor, who ruled together with Prince Gong. The following years are known as the Tongzhi Restoration, an effort to rebuild Confucian government after the wars. The idea of self-strengthening, ziqiang, was old: the phrase comes from the Book of Changes, the Yijing. In the 1840s the scholar Wei Yuan had urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to learn the superior techniques of the foreigners in order to control them, and in 1861 Feng Guifen presented to Zeng Guofan essays arguing that China must adopt Western methods to make itself strong.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imperial-examination.jpg" alt="Imperial examination" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">Imperial examination</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ming-Beamtenpr%C3%BCfungen1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qiu Ying (仇英) (attributed), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The self-strengtheners’ first aim was to save the dynasty, and the Confucian order it upheld, by acquiring the material sources of Western power. Officials spoke of learning the foreigners’ techniques in arms, ships and machines; from the 1870s they spoke too of “wealth and power”, fuqiang, recognising that arms needed industry, mines, transport and revenue behind them. Meanwhile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenxiang">Wenxiang</a> hoped to keep peace with the Western powers by observing the treaties strictly, to win time to build strength. The balance between old and new was later summed up by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong">Zhang Zhidong</a>, in his Exhortation to Study of 1898, as “Chinese learning for the substance, Western learning for practical use”: Western knowledge was to serve Chinese values and institutions, not to replace them.</p><p>Practical aims followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> was to train its own interpreters and diplomats instead of relying on foreigners; to translate Western works on mathematics, science, engineering and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, of which W. A. P. Martin’s version of Henry Wheaton’s Elements of International Law appeared in 1864; to build arsenals and shipyards that would end its dependence on foreign arms; and, from the 1870s, to found profit-making enterprises in shipping, mining and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraphs</a> that would win business back from foreign firms. Many were run as guandu shangban, “official supervision and merchant management”, which joined merchants’ capital to official patronage and control.</p><p>The movement met strong opposition. In 1867, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a> proposed that young holders of examination degrees study mathematics and astronomy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a>, the Grand Secretary Woren protested that the foundation of a state lay in propriety and righteousness, not in techniques, and that scholars should not take foreigners as their teachers. Railways were resisted as a threat to ancestral graves, to the harmony of the landscape and to the livelihoods of carters and boatmen. Above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a>, which shaped the education and careers of officials, stayed as it was, and the reformers themselves had no wish to change the political order they served.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal.jpg" alt="Jiangnan Arsenal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gun_transportation_at_Jiangnan_Arsenal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first phase, from 1861 to 1872, concentrated on arms and schools. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, created in 1861 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a>, acted as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s first office of foreign affairs, and the Imperial Maritime Customs Service, directed from 1863 by the British official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hart">Robert Hart</a>, gave the government a new and reliable revenue. In 1862 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a> opened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to train interpreters. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a> set up an arsenal at Anqing in 1861; in 1865 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, with machinery bought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yung-wing">Yung Wing</a>, and Li founded another arsenal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>; in 1866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zongtang">Zuo Zongtang</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou-navy-yard">Fuzhou Navy Yard</a>, with French help and a naval school. From 1868 the Jiangnan Arsenal’s translation department put Western works on science and engineering into Chinese.</p><p>In the second phase, from 1872 to 1885, the emphasis widened to wealth. Li Hongzhang, governor-general of Zhili from 1870, sponsored the China Merchants’ Steam Navigation Company in 1872, the Kaiping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines in 1877 and the Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">Telegraph</a> Administration in 1881. In 1872 the first boys of the Chinese Educational Mission left for schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>; conservative pressure ended the mission in 1881. China opened legations abroad, beginning with Guo Songtao’s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1877. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>’s expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1874 and its annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a> in 1879 revealed a new danger close to home. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-french-war">Sino-French War</a> a French squadron destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> fleet and bombarded the navy yard at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> in August 1884, although Qing armies fought well on land.</p><p>In the third phase, from 1885 to 1895, a Navy Board was created and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a>, with two German-built battleships, was formally established in 1888 under Admiral Ding Ruchang. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong">Zhang Zhidong</a>, governor-general at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> from 1889, built ironworks and an arsenal at Hanyang. Then rivalry over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> led to war with Japan in 1894. The Beiyang Fleet was defeated at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a> in September 1894; Japanese armies took Lüshun in November and Weihaiwei in February 1895, where the remnant of the fleet surrendered and Ding Ruchang took his own life. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a>, negotiated by Li Hongzhang, ended the war on 17 April 1895.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang.jpg" alt="Li Hongzhang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Hung_Chang_in_1896(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Russell &amp; Sons, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At court the movement depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a>, who headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zongli-yamen">Zongli Yamen</a>, and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> Grand Councillor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenxiang">Wenxiang</a>, its leading working minister until his death in 1876. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>, regent for the Tongzhi and then the Guangxu emperors, approved many projects but balanced the reformers against their conservative critics, and in 1884 she dismissed Prince Gong. The provincial leaders carried most of the work. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>, victor over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion">Taiping</a>, founded the first arsenals and sponsored the translation of Western books before his death in 1872. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a>, his protégé, dominated the movement for a quarter of a century from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>, founding arsenals, enterprises, schools and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a> and conducting much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s diplomacy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zongtang">Zuo Zongtang</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou-navy-yard">Fuzhou Navy Yard</a> before leaving to fight in the north-west, where he later reconquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>; Shen Baozhen directed the yard after him; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhidong">Zhang Zhidong</a> built industries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubei">Hubei</a>.</p><p>Around them worked specialists of a new kind. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yung-wing">Yung Wing</a>, also known as Rong Hong, the first Chinese graduate of an American university, at Yale in 1854, bought machinery for Jiangnan and led the Educational Mission. The scholars Xu Shou and Hua Hengfang translated Western science at Jiangnan with the Englishman John Fryer, and W. A. P. Martin taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a>, which he later headed. Writers who knew the treaty ports, such as the journalist Wang Tao and the merchant Zheng Guanying, urged bolder change. Foreign advisers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hart">Robert Hart</a> and the Frenchman Prosper Giquel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a>, played a large part.</p><p>The movement also had its workers and students. Thousands of craftsmen and labourers worked in the arsenals and dockyards, and graduates of the Fuzhou school went on to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. One of them, Yan Fu, studied at the Royal Naval College at Greenwich and later translated Thomas Huxley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a> into Chinese. Among the boys of the Educational Mission were Zhan Tianyou, who later built the railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangjiakou">Zhangjiakou</a>, and Tang Shaoyi, the first premier of the Republic of China.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki.jpg" alt="Treaty of Shimonoseki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japan_China_Peace_Treaty_17_April_1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Imaging, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the 1890s the movement had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> arsenals that made rifles, artillery and ammunition, shipyards that built steamships, a steamship company that competed with foreign firms on the coast and the Yangzi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> network linking the main cities, the beginnings of railways and of modern textile and iron industries, schools of languages, science and military and naval training, a body of translated Western learning, and a navy that on paper was among the strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. A generation of officers, engineers, interpreters and diplomats had been trained.</p><p>But the achievements were limited and scattered. The projects depended on the energy of individual governors, who competed for funds and kept control of their own enterprises and forces: the northern and southern fleets were never united, and in 1894 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-fleet">Beiyang Fleet</a> fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> largely alone. Many enterprises relied on imported machines, materials and foreign experts, and suffered from official interference, nepotism and corruption. The central government lacked the revenue and authority to direct a national programme, and some of the money meant for the navy is said to have gone into rebuilding the Summer Palace for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a>, though historians debate how much. The examinations, the bureaucracy and the court were left essentially unchanged.</p><p>The war with Japan exposed these weaknesses. Japan, which since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> of 1868 had reformed its government, army, schools and economy together, defeated the Qing on land and sea. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> China recognised the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a> Islands and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> Peninsula, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/triple-intervention">Triple Intervention</a> soon forced Japan to return, and agreed to pay an indemnity of 200 million taels. The defeat discredited the policy of borrowing techniques alone and led reformers to demand changes in institutions, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days’ Reform</a> of 1898 and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a>, in the New Policies of the 1900s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform.jpg" alt="Hundred Days' Reform" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W._A._P._Martin_%26_faculty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For much of the 20th century the movement was judged a failure. Chinese reformers after 1895, and later historians, set it against the success of Meiji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and in the People’s Republic the yangwu, or “foreign affairs”, movement was long treated as an attempt by officials of the landlord class to prop up the old order with foreign help. In the West, Mary C. Wright argued in 1957 that the Tongzhi Restoration showed the demands of modernisation to be at odds with those of Confucian stability, and Immanuel Hsü judged the movement superficial, concerned with military technology rather than with institutions or ideas.</p><p>Later historians have offered more varied judgements. Studies of particular enterprises, such as Albert Feuerwerker’s of the official and industrialist Sheng Xuanhuai, showed both what could be achieved and what held it back. Others have stressed politics: the rivalry of factions at court, the fiscal weakness of the centre and the competition between regional leaders. Benjamin Elman has argued that the arsenals and schools made real technical progress, and that the defeat of 1895, which owed more to command, coordination and politics than to backward science, led contemporaries and later historians to judge the whole effort too harshly; some scholars have even called it a near-success. The comparison with Japan remains central to these debates. Both countries began to adopt Western technology in the 1860s. In Japan, however, the reforms were directed by a new central government that had overthrown the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and was ready to remake every institution, whereas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> they were left largely to provincial officials serving an unchanged dynasty and a court divided over how far change should go.</p><p>Its legacy was concrete as well as intellectual. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a> grew into one of China’s great shipyards, and the China Merchants’ company survives as a business group today. The schools and missions trained men who served the late Qing and the early Republic as engineers, officers, translators and diplomats. And the movement opened a debate that ran through the following century, about how much China could take from the West, and how, without ceasing to be itself.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Self-Strengthening Movement of 1861–1895 was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>’s first sustained attempt to meet Western power by adopting Western arms, ships, industry and learning while keeping the Confucian order. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeng-guofan">Zeng Guofan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zongtang">Zuo Zongtang</a>, it built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>’s first modern arsenals, shipyards, enterprises and schools, but defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895 exposed the limits of reform without institutional change.</em></p>

